Each of these checkpoints is an oxygen valve. The system opens these valves for some people and keeps them closed for others. Your target user—the Lagos fashion seller making four hundred thousand naira monthly, the Port Harcourt beauty vendor with fifty products, the Abuja electronics reseller managing orders in WhatsApp—these people are on the wrong side of closed valves. The system has decided they're too small, too informal, too risky, or too expensive to serve.

So what did they do? They found the cracks in the system. Instagram doesn't require you to be a registered business. WhatsApp doesn't check your creditworthiness. Bank transfers don't need merchant accounts. They built businesses in the spaces between the organs, in the gaps where the system's control mechanisms don't reach.

But here's what you've recognized that they haven't fully articulated: building in the cracks is exhausting, chaotic, and fragile. You're succeeding despite the infrastructure, not because of it. You're spending five hours weekly copying product information between platforms. You're losing sales because customers can't find your full catalog. You're tracking orders in your head or in notebooks. You're one phone crash away from losing your entire customer database.

Tonstores doesn't just organize this chaos. It **legitimizes and professionalizes business models that the traditional system refuses to support**. You're giving infrastructure to people who were told they don't deserve infrastructure yet.

